 ## Abstract
 
-End-to-end argument mining jointly identifies component spans, component types, and argumentative relations from raw text. Components are interdependent: paraphrasing a component during training may alter its relations to surrounding components, while changing its span or type during inference may invalidate existing relations. Increasing the number of textual variants or editing predictions independently does not explicitly address these dependencies. Building on the author's prior work on conclusion-specific modeling, multi-candidate reranking, and bounded repair, this thesis develops two complementary methods for preserving relational supervision and restoring prediction dependencies.
-
-The first method is relation-aware augmentation learning for conclusion components. Only designated component spans may be paraphrased. Legal endpoint combinations within the editable set and across its boundary are checked for preservation of both annotated relations and no-relation states. Deterministic annotation transfer establishes component correspondence after text replacement; detected semantic drift leads to rejection of the entire variant. Building on the inherited conclusion-specific modeling, paired component and relation supervision connects accepted textual variations to the shared generation representation. Source-document normalization controls the influence of multiple variants from the same document.
-
-The second method, MCR-BR-J, repairs predicted argument graphs while keeping the input text unchanged. After multi-candidate reranking, compatible component-set replacements support insertion, deletion, span or type changes, splitting, and merging within a fixed candidate universe. A replacement and the reconstruction of all affected relations form one transaction, which is accepted or rolled back as a whole under fixed scores, global structural constraints, and explicit budgets. The scorer estimates the correctness of complete objects, including spans, types, and relation endpoints. A cost-sensitive utility provides the comparison criterion. Conditional analyses establish annotation transport, local utility differences, and finite termination without guaranteeing improvement in true F1.
-
-The evaluation protocol separates the contributions of training-time learning and inference-time repair on AAEC and CDCP, retaining CDCP multi-premise relations as native hyperedges. It includes related augmentation methods, matched-data loss ablations, sequential and joint repair under identical candidates and scores, global structured decoding, candidate recovery, harmful edits, and computational cost. Prior results and diagnostics establish the research basis. Numerical scenarios in Chapter 5 are writing placeholders, not measurements; new empirical claims require reproducible runs and strict evaluation.
+End-to-end argument mining identifies argumentative components and their relations directly from raw text. Dependencies between components and relations create two related problems. During training, a local paraphrase may alter the argumentative role of a component and invalidate inherited labels. During inference, changing a predicted span or type may invalidate the endpoints of associated relations. Building on prior work on conclusion-specific modeling, multi-candidate reranking, and bounded repair, this thesis studies supervision preservation during training and structural recovery during inference.
+
+The first method is relation-aware augmentation learning for conclusion components. It defines an affected relation domain from component dependencies and checks both annotated relations and no-relation states within and across the editable set. Annotation transfer establishes correspondences between original and augmented documents. Paired component and relation learning then connects accepted text variations to the shared generation representation.
+
+The second method is dependency-closed argument graph repair driven by multiple candidates. It leaves the input text unchanged and combines compatible component-set replacement with reconstruction of all affected relations. Each proposed transaction is accepted or rolled back as a whole under complete-object scores, global structural constraints, and explicit editing budgets. Set replacement represents insertion, deletion, span or type changes, splitting, and merging. Conditional analysis characterizes local utility differences and bounded execution without guaranteeing improvement in true extraction F1.
+
+The evaluation framework uses AAEC and CDCP, retaining CDCP multi-premise relations as native hyperedges. Independent comparisons, a training-by-inference factorial design, harmful-edit analysis, and cost measurements separate the two methods and their combined effects. Prior results establish the research basis. Values marked with a P suffix in this revision are manually specified placeholders, not measurements; empirical conclusions for the new methods remain pending.
 
 Keywords: End-to-End Argument Mining; Relation-Aware Augmentation; Conclusion Components; Dependency Closure; Graph Repair; Cost-Sensitive Scoring
 
